<section class="band" id="tips">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="niche-plans-head">
      <div class="eyebrow">Tips</div>
      <h2>What makes a therapist website <em>work</em></h2>
      <p class="lead">Whether you're just starting a private practice or you've had one for years, these four things matter most.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="tips">
      <div class="tip">
        <span class="n">1</span>
        <h3>It should feel like you</h3>
        <p>Before you start, look at other therapists' sites in your area and note what you like and don't. Fonts, colors and photos change how a site feels more than most people expect. Warm colors tend to feel energetic, and cool colors tend to feel calm.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tip">
        <span class="n">2</span>
        <h3>Have one clear next step</h3>
        <p>Every page should lead to one place: your contact form or booking page. Most visitors look at your homepage, then your About or Services page, and then decide. Some skip straight to booking.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tip">
        <span class="n">3</span>
        <h3>Help people trust you before they meet you</h3>
        <p>Share your credentials, training and how you work, so someone can picture what a session with you is like. A clear, friendly photo of you helps too.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tip">
        <span class="n">4</span>
        <h3>Show what you specialize in</h3>
        <p>People usually come looking for help with something specific, like ADHD, OCD or their marriage. A page for each specialty tells them right away that you can help, and it gives Google a page to show for that search.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <figure class="tips-photo"><img src="/assets/img/pages/private-practice-therapist-websites/therapist-reading.jpg" alt="A woman reading a book" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></figure>
  </div>
</section>

